  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Locked is one-way",
      "items": [
        "Two legal changes and only two: supersession, and a dated erratum",
        "Everything above an Errata heading stays byte-identical",
        "Editing a locked file in place is a CI failure, because it destroys the record of what was true when the code was written"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Erratum or supersession",
      "items": [
        "A wrong FACT — a miscount, a stale figure — gets an erratum, and the decision is unaffected",
        "A wrong DECISION gets superseded",
        "Reaching for the cheaper instrument on the wrong one is how a record stops being one"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "Two types may never lock",
      "items": [
        "A bet exists to be contradicted by evidence on a date already written down",
        "Locking one would make the word mean two incompatible things",
        "An RFC is a design under discussion, not a rule"
      ]
    }
  ]
